<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-expressions-with-fractions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Expressions with Fractions</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>Add, subtract, multiply, and divide fractions.</p></li>
			<li><p>Determine when common denominators are required and when they are not.</p></li>
			<li><p>Determine when cancelling is permissible in a fraction and when it isn't.</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>

	<subsection>
		<title>Order of Operations</title>

    <assemblage permid="fraction-meaning">
      <title>Representing Addition Geometrically</title>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are positive real numbers.
        Then <m>a+b</m> is geometrically represented by the length of the stick made by 
        gluing a stick of length <m>a</m> to a stick of length <m>b</m> .        

        <figure xml:id="fig-fraction-meaning">
          <caption>A picture of <m>a+b</m></caption>
          <image width="50%">
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (3,0);
                  \fill[black] (0,0) circle (2pt);
                  \fill[black] (3,0) circle (2pt);
                  \node at (1.5,-0.3) {$a$};

                  \draw[thick] (3.2,0) -- (5.2,0);
                  \fill[black] (3.2,0) circle (2pt);
                  \fill[black] (5.2,0) circle (2pt);
                  \node at (4.2,-0.3) {$b$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>




  <p>The top and bottom of a fraction are in (hidden) parentheses. Affects calculator input.</p>

  <p>Mixed fractions, improper fractions, decimal answers. Why improper fractions are the focus.</p>
  <p>Two correct (and one incorrect) interpretation of <m>5 \frac{3}{7}</m>.</p>



</section>